  "title": "Erdős Problem #536",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $\\epsilon>0$ and $N$ be sufficiently large. Is it true that if $A\\subseteq \\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$ has size at least $\\epsilon N$ then there must be distinct $a,b,c\\in A$ such that $ [a,b]=[b,c]=[a,c], $ where $[a,b]$ denotes the least common multiple?",
